NAME
    vi - modal screen editor

SYNOPSIS
    vi [FILE]

DESCRIPTION
    A vi-style modal editor. Loads FILE into the buffer, draws the
    screen via direct sys_tty_cursor positioning, and reads keys in
    TTY_RAW mode.

MODES
    Normal mode (default on start)
    Insert mode (entered via i / I / a / A / o / O)
    Command-line mode (entered via : / / / ?)

KEYS (normal mode)
    h j k l         cursor left / down / up / right
    w / b           jump word forward / backward
    0 / $           start / end of line
    gg / G          first / last line of file
    NG              jump to line N (e.g. `42G`)

    i / a           enter insert mode (at cursor / after cursor)
    I / A           enter insert mode (start / end of current line)
    o / O           open line below / above + enter insert mode
    x               delete char under cursor
    dd              delete current line
    yy              yank current line to register
    p / P           paste register after / before cursor

    u               undo last edit (session 86; 32-deep ring)

COUNT PREFIXES (session 86)
    A leading number repeats the next command:
      5j           down 5 lines
      10w          forward 10 words
      3x           delete 3 chars
      5dd          delete 5 lines (yanked into register)
      3yy          yank 3 lines
      42G          jump to line 42
    `0` alone is still the "jump to col 0" motion; once a count is
    nonzero, `0` becomes a regular digit (so `10j` works).

SEARCH
    /pat          forward search for `pat` (literal substring)
    ?pat          backward search
    n             repeat last search in its original direction
    N             repeat last search FLIPPED (forward becomes
                  backward and vice versa)

COMMAND-LINE MODE (:)
    :w [FILE]                write to FILE (default: loaded file)
    :q                       quit (refuses if unsaved)
    :q!                      quit unconditionally
    :wq                      write then quit
    :N                       jump to line N
    :s/old/new/              replace first match on current line
    :s/old/new/g             replace ALL matches on current line
    :%s/old/new/             replace first match on every line
    :%s/old/new/g            replace every match on every line

EXAMPLES
    vi hello.txt
    /TODO        n    n              # walk through TODO occurrences
    :%s/foo/bar/g                    # replace foo with bar everywhere
    5dd                              # delete 5 lines into the register
    p                                # paste them somewhere else
    u                                # undo

EXIT STATUS
    0   clean quit
    1   command syntax error or write failure

SEE ALSO
    ed(1), sh(1)

NOTES
    - Search patterns are LITERAL SUBSTRINGS, not regex (matches the
      rest of this OS).
    - :s/old/new/ doesn't support '/' inside `old` or `new` — escape
      via a different pattern.
    - Cursor positioning uses SYS_TTY_CURSOR, which updates the
      framebuffer and VGA grid but not the host terminal's idea
      of cursor over -serial stdio. The QEMU graphical window
      renders vi correctly; the serial side gets a redraw-only
      view. Limitation acknowledged.
    - Undo is bounded at 32 entries; older changes silently fall
      off the end of the ring.
